 The experimental target is ERA5-derived regional-mean precipitation over Dublin, assessed within a common multivariate reanalysis framework rather than as a station-specific operational forecast. ERA5 supplies a long, temporally continuous record of meteorological fields on a common grid, supporting consistent construction of predictors and targets \citep{hersbach2020era5}. This describes the scope and methodological rationale of the present comparison, not evidence that reanalysis precipitation is more accurate than gauge observations. \citet{otero2023intercomparison} likewise used ERA5 predictors and precipitation fields for an architecture intercomparison, but their European daily contemporaneous modelling task differs from our six-hour-ahead hourly target. ERA5 precipitation is a model-generated estimate; consistency within its framework does not establish accuracy against independent rainfall observations.
 
-Meteorological conditions over the study region were characterized using the ERA5 single-level reanalysis dataset from ECMWF. We used the Copernicus Climate Data Store (CDS) product ``ERA5 hourly data on single levels'' with the ``reanalysis'' product option. \textcolor{red}{The original ERA5 product has an hourly temporal resolution. We selected only the hourly values at 00:00, 06:00, 12:00, and 18:00 UTC, giving a 6-hour sampling interval; no 6-hour accumulation, temporal averaging, or temporal interpolation was performed.} Following data-quality screening, the complete period from January 2004 to December 2021 was retained, yielding 26,300 consecutive timestamps with no missing values in the variables used for subsequent analysis. Eleven meteorological variables were initially considered as candidate predictors: 10-metre u-component of wind (u10), 10-metre v-component of wind (v10), 2-metre dewpoint temperature (d2m), 2-metre temperature (t2m), surface pressure (sp), evaporation (e), maximum total precipitation rate (mxtpr), minimum total precipitation rate (mntpr), total cloud cover (tcc), surface net solar radiation (ssr), and large-scale precipitation (lsp). Total precipitation (tp) was used as the prediction target, as summarized in Table~\ref{tab:variables}.
+Meteorological conditions over the study region were characterized using the ERA5 single-level reanalysis dataset from ECMWF. We used the Copernicus Climate Data Store (CDS) product ``ERA5 hourly data on single levels'' with the ``reanalysis'' product option. \textcolor{red}{The original ERA5 product has an hourly temporal resolution. We selected only the hourly values at 00:00, 06:00, 12:00, and 18:00 UTC, giving a 6-hour sampling interval; no 6-hour accumulation, temporal averaging, or temporal interpolation was performed.} Following data-quality screening, the complete period from January 2004 to December 2021 was retained, yielding 26,300 consecutive timestamps with no missing values in the variables used for subsequent analysis. Eleven meteorological variables were initially considered as candidate predictors: 10-metre u-component of wind (u10), 10-metre v-component of wind (v10), 2-metre dewpoint temperature (d2m), 2-metre temperature (t2m), surface pressure (sp), evaporation (e), maximum total precipitation rate (mxtpr), minimum total precipitation rate (mntpr), total cloud cover (tcc), surface net solar radiation (ssr), and large-scale precipitation (lsp). \revminor{These eleven candidates comprised the non-target meteorological variables available in the original ERA5 extract. They describe complementary aspects of the atmospheric and surface state: near-surface temperature and moisture conditions (t2m and d2m), wind and pressure conditions (u10, v10 and sp), cloud cover (tcc), surface radiative and moisture exchanges (ssr and e, where e is the modelled actual evaporation rather than potential evaporation), and recent precipitation characteristics (lsp, mxtpr and mntpr). Previous data-driven rainfall prediction studies have considered observational analogues of temperature, dew point, wind, pressure, cloud cover and solar radiation, although these are not necessarily identical to the ERA5 fields used here \citep{manandhar2019data,rahman2022rainfall,gao2025applying}. The precipitation-related variables lsp, mxtpr and mntpr instead provide information about preceding-hour large-scale precipitation and the within-hour extrema of total precipitation rate \citep{ecmwf_era5_documentation}. The inclusion of all eleven variables defines an exploratory candidate pool rather than presupposing that each variable contributes independent predictive information; their associations with the forecast target were subsequently examined through training-period screening and lead-time diagnostics.} Total precipitation (tp) was used as the prediction target, as summarized in Table~\ref{tab:variables}.
 
 For each timestamp, the meteorological variables were spatially averaged across the 25 ERA5 grid points within the Dublin study domain to construct a regional mean time series. Each retained total-precipitation value represents the accumulation during the hour ending at its timestamp. Total precipitation was multiplied by 1000 to convert metres to millimetres. This length-unit conversion does not change the accumulation window. Dividing the one-hour amount by its one-hour duration gives the hourly-mean rate in mm\,h$^{-1}$; its numerical value is unchanged. The prediction target and all precipitation-valued forecast metrics are reported in mm\,h$^{-1}$ throughout. No temporal interpolation or forward-filling was applied because the retained 2004--2021 period formed a complete and continuous 6-hourly time series. The resulting regional time series was chronologically divided into training (70\%), validation (15\%), and testing (15\%) subsets to preserve temporal ordering and prevent future observations from influencing model development. Standardization was performed using statistics estimated exclusively from the training subset, and the same transformation was subsequently applied to the validation and test subsets.
 
